[FINAL-TEXT] I now have complete confirmation. Let me summarize the evidence:

1. **All IPs are reachable** — ping and HTTP to every data-center server succeed (0% loss, 200 OK).
2. **DNS queries return REFUSED** — `dig @10.200.0.2 web_server_0` gets `status: REFUSED` with a "recursion not available" warning.
3. **Authoritative "local" zone works** — `dig @10.200.0.2 web0.local` returns 10.200.0.3, and `curl http://web0.local/` loads correctly.
4. **Root cause in named.conf** — `/etc/bind/named.conf` on dns_server was modified **today (Aug 5 22:24)** and contains `recursion no;`. The standard Debian files (`named.conf.options`, `named.conf.local`, `named.conf.default-zones`) are all dated Jul 27 2024 and untouched. The injected named.conf does not include any of them.
5. **No search domain on the host** — `resolv.conf` has only `nameserver 10.200.0.2` with no `search local` directive, so all bare-name queries go out without the `.local` suffix, land outside the authoritative zone, and are refused.

---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on dns_server in the BIND9 configuration. The file
/etc/bind/named.conf was replaced or rewritten today (Aug 5 22:24) with a
stripped-down version that sets "recursion no;" and does not include the
standard Debian helper files (named.conf.options, named.conf.local,
named.conf.default-zones). The older files (dated Jul 27 2024) remain on
disk but are no longer loaded.

Because recursion is disabled, BIND refuses every query for a name it is
not already authoritative for. The server is only authoritative for the
"local" zone (web0.local … web3.local, ns1.local). The hosts have
/etc/resolv.conf entries pointing exclusively to 10.200.0.2 but carry no
"search local" domain suffix. This means every application-level lookup
(e.g. "web_server_0", any FQDN outside the "local" zone) receives a DNS
REFUSED response. From the user's perspective every named data-center
resource appears completely unreachable, even though layer-3 connectivity
is fully intact — direct IP pings and HTTP requests to all four web servers
(10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6) and the DNS server itself (10.200.0.2) succeed
with zero loss.

The root cause is that "recursion no;" was injected into
/etc/bind/named.conf on dns_server, disabling recursive resolution for all
clients.

Corrective fix: on dns_server, restore /etc/bind/named.conf to the
standard Debian form that includes named.conf.options, named.conf.local,
and named.conf.default-zones, and ensure named.conf.options contains
"recursion yes;" (or configure explicit forwarders). Then reload BIND9
("rndc reload" or "systemctl reload named"). Optionally also add "search
local" to each host's resolv.conf so short names in the "local" zone
resolve without an FQDN.
=== END ===
```